OK?

00:13:01.220 --> 00:13:04.970
Why is it a two-dimensional
subspace?

00:13:05.240 --> 00:13:08.760
It's because one of the main
facts we learned on Tuesday

00:13:08.760 --> 00:13:11.200
was that the dimension of the
row space and column space are

00:13:11.200 --> 00:13:13.740
always the same for any matrix.

00:13:13.740 --> 00:13:14.740
OK?

00:13:16.080 --> 00:13:19.580
What that means is no matter how
hard you try,

00:13:19.580 --> 00:13:24.830
only two of these four rows can
be linearly independent at once.

00:13:25.000 --> 00:13:28.900
OK, and I encourage you to
experiment to figure that out.

00:13:28.900 --> 00:13:31.480
And let me just restate one of
the important facts.

00:13:31.480 --> 00:13:32.480
Important,

00:13:33.620 --> 00:13:39.560
the rank of A

00:13:39.560 --> 00:13:44.480
is always equal to the rank of A
transpose,

00:13:44.480 --> 00:13:50.340
meaning the dimension of the
column

00:13:50.340 --> 00:13:52.590
space of A

00:13:53.320 --> 00:13:58.200
is always equal to the dimension
of the column space of A

00:13:58.200 --> 00:13:59.200
transpose,

00:14:00.440 --> 00:14:01.440
OK?

00:14:02.080 --> 00:14:05.840
So one thing that I glossed over
at the end of

00:14:05.840 --> 00:14:10.980
Tuesday's lecture is that,
suppose we have a matrix that's

00:14:10.980 --> 00:14:11.980
tall.

00:14:12.620 --> 00:14:16.370
Can its columns be linearly
independent?

00:14:19.500 --> 00:14:20.360
That is,

00:14:20.360 --> 00:14:22.640
by that I mean, can all of its
columns together be linearly

00:14:22.640 --> 00:14:23.640
independent?

00:14:23.680 --> 00:14:24.920
Is that possible?

00:14:24.920 --> 00:14:26.560
Yeah, it's possible.
Well, it's not guaranteed.

00:14:26.560 --> 00:14:28.060
They could all be the same
column, for all we know.

00:14:28.060 --> 00:14:30.560
But it's possible that they're
all linearly independent.

00:14:30.560 --> 00:14:34.420
But it's not possible that its
rows are linearly independent.

00:14:34.420 --> 00:14:35.320
Why is that?

00:14:35.320 --> 00:14:38.680
Well, suppose, again, anytime
you're faced with a hypothetical

00:14:38.680 --> 00:14:40.960
like this, just make numbers up.

00:14:40.960 --> 00:14:43.580
I pretend n is 50 and d is 4.

00:14:43.580 --> 00:14:47.680
What is the max number of
linearly independent columns?

00:14:47.680 --> 00:14:48.680
4.

00:14:48.700 --> 00:14:50.820
They could all be linearly
independent.

00:14:50.820 --> 00:14:53.900
What is the max number of
linearly independent rows?

00:14:53.900 --> 00:14:56.360
Also 4.
But there's 50 rows total,

00:14:56.880 --> 00:14:59.620
meaning they can't all be
linearly independent.

00:14:59.620 --> 00:15:02.400
So if you have a tall matrix,

00:15:02.400 --> 00:15:06.820
the rows have to be linearly
dependent.

00:15:06.820 --> 00:15:09.580
Similarly, what if we have a
wide matrix?

00:15:09.580 --> 00:15:12.360
And let me write that here.

00:15:12.360 --> 00:15:14.610
Number of linearly.

00:15:40.380 --> 00:15:44.080
Do you see what I was getting at
with this example?

00:15:44.080 --> 00:15:48.040
If you have a tall matrix with
way more rows than columns, it

00:15:48.040 --> 00:15:50.160
doesn't have to be way more.

00:15:50.160 --> 00:15:52.820
The rows can't be linearly
independent, but the columns

00:15:52.820 --> 00:15:54.200
could be.

00:15:54.200 --> 00:15:59.180
What if I instead had a wide
matrix?

00:15:59.180 --> 00:16:01.960
These are less common in the
context of real data.

00:16:01.960 --> 00:16:03.800
Then what's the situation?

00:16:03.800 --> 00:16:07.000
Could the columns be linearly
independent?

00:16:07.000 --> 00:16:10.600
No, they can't, using the same
logic.

00:16:10.600 --> 00:16:13.460
We know the number of linearly
independent rows equals the

00:16:13.460 --> 00:16:16.200
number of linearly independent
columns.

00:16:16.200 --> 00:16:18.700
And here we have more columns
than rows.

00:16:18.700 --> 00:16:23.950
So even if every row is linearly
independent,

00:16:24.760 --> 00:16:27.940
the columns still can't be
linearly independent,

00:16:27.940 --> 00:16:30.620
because there are more columns.
There are too many columns.

00:16:30.620 --> 00:16:31.520
Again,

00:16:31.520 --> 00:16:34.520
a situation like this may come
numbers, 4 and 7.

00:16:34.520 --> 00:16:37.200
At most, you have four linearly
independent rows, which means

00:16:37.200 --> 00:16:38.940
you have four linearly
independent columns, but you

00:16:38.940 --> 00:16:40.660
have seven columns total, so
they're not all linearly

00:16:40.660 --> 00:16:41.640
independent.

00:16:41.640 --> 00:16:44.620
So the only way for the
dimensions to equal the size of

00:16:44.620 --> 00:16:49.580
the subspace is for it to be a
square matrix?

00:16:49.580 --> 00:16:54.660
Let me clarify here.
So you're saying the only way

00:16:54.660 --> 00:16:58.860
for the shape to be equal to the
size of the subspace for both

00:16:58.860 --> 00:17:03.360
the column space and the row
space?

00:17:03.420 --> 00:17:05.280
Yes, is for it to be square.

00:17:05.280 --> 00:17:05.900
Yes.

00:17:05.900 --> 00:17:07.800
So to put that into context
here,

00:17:07.800 --> 00:17:08.900
it's possible,

00:17:08.900 --> 00:17:13.580
again, if the rows are all
linearly independent, that the

00:17:13.580 --> 00:17:16.100
dimension of the row space and
column space here are both 4,

00:17:16.100 --> 00:17:18.180
which is equal to one of the
shape dimensions, but not both

00:17:18.180 --> 00:17:19.340
of them.

00:17:19.340 --> 00:17:24.590
Now, if we have a square matrix,
that's where I was getting at.

00:17:27.020 --> 00:17:28.220
Here's a question.

00:17:28.220 --> 00:17:31.160
Is it possible for all of the
columns to be linearly

00:17:31.160 --> 00:17:35.660
independent, but the rows to be
linearly dependent?

00:17:35.680 --> 00:17:37.860
Is that possible? Can the
columns be independent, but the

00:17:37.860 --> 00:17:40.110
rows be dependent?

00:17:41.340 --> 00:17:45.120
I'm seeing a bunch of no's.
Why are you saying no?

00:17:45.120 --> 00:17:47.960
You're right, but tell me why.

00:17:47.960 --> 00:17:48.700
Yeah.

00:17:48.700 --> 00:17:50.580
Because if it's square,

00:17:50.580 --> 00:17:53.000
that means there's the same
number of columns as rows.

00:17:53.000 --> 00:17:54.860
Good.
So if you're saying that one is

00:17:54.860 --> 00:17:56.200
linearly independent,

00:17:56.200 --> 00:17:58.400
then it has to have the same
rank in the other.

00:17:58.400 --> 00:17:59.400
Good.

00:18:01.740 --> 00:18:06.240
Then if all columns are linearly
independent,

00:18:12.960 --> 00:18:18.900
and then all rows must be as
well,

00:18:20.940 --> 00:18:23.190
and vice versa.

00:18:26.060 --> 00:18:29.240
And the short answer for
invertibility

00:18:29.240 --> 00:18:34.140
is that a square matrix has an
inverse when all of its columns

00:18:34.140 --> 00:18:36.390
are linearly independent.

00:18:37.460 --> 00:18:40.160
But that alone tells you that
there's lots of other ways of

00:18:40.160 --> 00:18:42.380
defining it.
Because you could also say it's

00:18:42.380 --> 00:18:45.760
invertible when all of its rows
are linearly independent, or

00:18:45.760 --> 00:18:49.260
when its rank is equal to its
number of columns or rows.

00:18:49.260 --> 00:18:51.420
We're going to cover that in a
little bit, but that's where

00:18:51.420 --> 00:18:52.920
we're headed.

00:18:53.720 --> 00:18:54.720
OK.

00:18:54.900 --> 00:18:59.700
What I want to get at now is the
idea of this other subspace

00:18:59.700 --> 00:19:02.600
built into this matrix.
And this sort of relates to the

00:19:02.600 --> 00:19:06.350
last question in yesterday's lab
worksheet.

00:19:10.580 --> 00:19:14.580
I want to copy this matrix over
first.

00:19:14.580 --> 00:19:17.380
Let me open yesterday's lab
worksheet to draw my point home

00:19:17.380 --> 00:19:18.380
here.

00:19:21.300 --> 00:19:22.840
Do you all remember this
