00:22:17.000 --> 00:22:19.240
just negative 3 over 2.

00:22:19.240 --> 00:22:20.240
Sorry,

00:22:20.340 --> 00:22:22.800
negative 2 over 3 times its
second column.

00:22:22.800 --> 00:22:24.700
The columns are scalar multiples
of each other.

00:22:24.700 --> 00:22:27.700
That's the key here.

00:22:37.080 --> 00:22:41.680
Do we see that A's columns are
scalar multiples of each other?

00:22:41.680 --> 00:22:44.680
And as a result,

00:22:46.060 --> 00:22:50.960
I was able to find a vector that
I can multiply the matrix by,

00:22:50.960 --> 00:22:55.040
and what came out was the zero
vector.

00:22:55.040 --> 00:22:56.520
And because that happened,

00:22:56.520 --> 00:23:00.280
this is telling me that A's
columns are not linearly

00:23:00.280 --> 00:23:01.280
independent.

00:23:03.420 --> 00:23:06.420
Because if they were linearly
independent,

00:23:06.420 --> 00:23:09.020
well you tell me, before midterm
one I gave you a formal

00:23:09.020 --> 00:23:11.720
definition of what it meant for
vectors to be linearly

00:23:11.720 --> 00:23:13.480
independent.
What was that formal definition?

00:23:16.880 --> 00:23:18.620
Yeah, it had something to do
with the zero vector.

00:23:18.620 --> 00:23:21.300
The formal definition of linear
independence said a collection

00:23:21.300 --> 00:23:24.420
of vectors is linearly
independent if the only way you

00:23:24.420 --> 00:23:27.660
can make the zero vector with
them is by taking zero of every

00:23:27.660 --> 00:23:28.440
one.

00:23:28.440 --> 00:23:31.240
If there is any other way you
can make the zero vector,

00:23:31.240 --> 00:23:34.380
then the vectors are not
linearly independent.

00:23:34.380 --> 00:23:36.040
Does that sound familiar?

00:23:36.040 --> 00:23:36.620
Okay,

00:23:36.620 --> 00:23:39.840
what this is telling us here is
that A's columns are not

00:23:39.840 --> 00:23:43.240
linearly independent, because I
was able to make the zero vector

00:23:43.240 --> 00:23:47.360
using a non-trivial linear
combination of them.

00:23:47.400 --> 00:23:49.840
I took three of the first and 2
of the second, and what came out

00:23:49.840 --> 00:23:52.840
was a 0 vector.

00:23:54.240 --> 00:24:00.160
3 col 1 plus 2 col 2 equals a

00:24:00.160 --> 00:24:01.600
0 vector.

00:24:01.600 --> 00:24:06.180
So the columns are not linearly

00:24:06.180 --> 00:24:07.800
independent.

00:24:10.680 --> 00:24:12.440
Do we remember that formal
definition of linear

00:24:12.440 --> 00:24:13.440
independence?

00:24:13.440 --> 00:24:16.200
Your vectors are linearly
independent if the only way you

00:24:16.200 --> 00:24:18.800
can use them to make the 0
vector is by taking 0 of each

00:24:18.800 --> 00:24:19.500
one.

00:24:19.500 --> 00:24:22.100
Here, the columns of A are not
linearly independent because I

00:24:22.100 --> 00:24:27.400
was able to find a vector that I
can multiply A by to get the 0

00:24:27.400 --> 00:24:28.880
vector.
Or equivalently, I was able to

00:24:28.880 --> 00:24:33.060
find a linear combination of
them that equals the 0 vector.

00:24:33.060 --> 00:24:35.880
But that linear combination
didn't involve just taking 0 of

00:24:35.880 --> 00:24:37.380
each one.

00:24:37.540 --> 00:24:38.540
OK.

00:24:38.660 --> 00:24:41.180
I want to see how that logic
extends here

00:24:41.180 --> 00:24:43.840
to the matrix we started class
