You have a 1 and a 1, so this
should be a 1.

00:57:58.800 --> 00:57:59.800
OK.

00:58:02.260 --> 00:58:06.800
Can you think of how to write
this matrix B as an outer

00:58:06.800 --> 00:58:07.760
product,

00:58:07.760 --> 00:58:10.200
one vector times another?

00:58:10.200 --> 00:58:14.700
But instead of row then column,
we're doing column then row.

00:58:17.200 --> 00:58:19.450
Any ideas here?

00:58:20.000 --> 00:58:20.400
Yeah.

00:58:20.400 --> 00:58:22.660
1, 2 for the first one.
Good.

00:58:22.660 --> 00:58:24.240
The second one is 1, 2, 3.

00:58:24.240 --> 00:58:25.240
Good.

00:58:25.420 --> 00:58:29.760
The way that this outer product
representation of a rank 1

00:58:29.760 --> 00:58:33.940
matrix works is that the row you
provide

00:58:33.940 --> 00:58:36.920
should be such that every row of
the matrix is a scalar multiple

00:58:36.920 --> 00:58:38.240
of that one row.

00:58:38.240 --> 00:58:39.700
And the column you provide,

00:58:39.700 --> 00:58:42.240
every column in the matrix, is a
scalar multiple of that one

00:58:42.240 --> 00:58:43.240
column.

00:58:43.320 --> 00:58:46.620
So every column in the matrix is
1, 2 times something.

00:58:46.620 --> 00:58:48.020
It's 1, 2 times 1,

00:58:48.020 --> 00:58:49.240
1, 2 times 2,

00:58:49.240 --> 00:58:50.960
and 1, 2 times 3.

00:58:50.960 --> 00:58:54.710
And those scalars come from
here, one, two, and three.

00:58:55.820 --> 00:59:00.320
So this is what's called an
outer product.

00:59:00.380 --> 00:59:03.660
And this is something that will
come up throughout the semester.

00:59:03.660 --> 00:59:09.400
An outer product always results
in a rank one matrix.

00:59:09.940 --> 00:59:14.660
And a rank one matrix can always
be written as an outer product.

00:59:14.660 --> 00:59:17.200
Turns out a rank,

00:59:17.200 --> 00:59:19.920
some other number than one, like
a rank seven matrix,

00:59:19.920 --> 00:59:24.640
can be written as a sum of seven
outer products.

00:59:24.640 --> 00:59:27.120
Okay, so every matrix can be
decomposed into the sum of

00:59:27.120 --> 00:59:28.480
several outer products.

00:59:28.480 --> 00:59:32.400
But that's a very rich idea and
one that we will come to at the

00:59:32.400 --> 00:59:33.800
end of this semester.

00:59:33.800 --> 00:59:35.560
Okay, that wasn't really the
point here.

00:59:35.560 --> 00:59:37.220
The rank of this matrix is one,

00:59:37.220 --> 00:59:41.720
so the dimension of its null
space

00:59:41.860 --> 00:59:44.110
is then what?

00:59:44.760 --> 00:59:48.510
What did the rank nullity
theorem tell me this would be?

00:59:49.340 --> 00:59:50.240
Yeah,

00:59:50.240 --> 00:59:51.320
two, good.

00:59:51.320 --> 00:59:54.320
So here's your job.

00:59:54.580 --> 01:00:00.480
Find one basis for

01:00:00.480 --> 01:00:03.120
the null space of this matrix B.

01:00:03.120 --> 01:00:05.060
There are infinitely many,

01:00:05.060 --> 01:00:10.120
but what is shared in common
amongst all of those basises for

01:00:10.120 --> 01:00:15.370
the null space of B? What is
true about all of them?

01:00:15.800 --> 01:00:18.720
How many vectors are in all of
them?

01:00:18.720 --> 01:00:21.880
Every basis for the null space
of B has how many vectors?

01:00:21.880 --> 01:00:22.180
Yeah.

01:00:22.180 --> 01:00:22.900
Two.

01:00:22.900 --> 01:00:23.900
Exactly.

01:00:26.400 --> 01:00:31.650
So try and find one of the
infinitely many bases

01:00:31.680 --> 01:00:36.180
for the null space of B.

01:02:03.800 --> 01:02:04.800
OK,

01:02:05.020 --> 01:02:07.380
how did you work on this?

01:02:07.380 --> 01:02:12.060
Give me one vector in the null
space of B, and tell me how you

01:02:12.060 --> 01:02:13.560
found it.

01:02:14.100 --> 01:02:18.100
How did you find one vector in
the null space of B?

01:02:18.100 --> 01:02:21.320
Again, what this means is you're
looking, I'm asking for a vector

01:02:21.320 --> 01:02:25.440
who, if I multiplied it by B,

01:02:25.440 --> 01:02:27.500
it would get turned into the
zero vector.

01:02:27.500 --> 01:02:29.200
What's one such vector?

01:02:29.200 --> 01:02:33.060
Well, I saw that the second
column is equal to 2 times the

01:02:33.060 --> 01:02:36.440
first column.
So I did 2, negative 1, and 0.

01:02:37.800 --> 01:02:38.800
Excellent.

01:02:41.960 --> 01:02:44.980
That's the first vector you
likely thought of.

01:02:44.980 --> 01:02:48.460
And it uses the fact that the
second column is just 2 times

01:02:48.460 --> 01:02:49.920
the first column.

01:02:49.920 --> 01:02:54.800
What that means is 2 times
column 1 minus 1 times column 2.

01:02:55.120 --> 01:02:57.340
You can leave column 3 alone.

01:02:57.340 --> 01:02:58.700
You get the 0 vector.

01:02:58.700 --> 01:02:59.700
OK.

01:03:00.140 --> 01:03:02.080
So one vector in your basis

01:03:02.080 --> 01:03:09.260
can

01:03:09.260 --> 01:03:11.660
be 2, negative 1, 0.

01:03:11.660 --> 01:03:15.140
Now we just need another vector
who is linearly independent from

01:03:15.140 --> 01:03:16.000
this first one.

01:03:16.000 --> 01:03:21.250
And how might you find that one?

01:03:22.580 --> 01:03:24.360
How about you find another one?
Yeah.

01:03:24.360 --> 01:03:26.800
I compared the first and the
second columns.

01:03:26.800 --> 01:03:29.260
So you get negative 3, 0, 1.

01:03:29.260 --> 01:03:30.140
Sure.

01:03:30.140 --> 01:03:31.220
Negative 3,

01:03:31.220 --> 01:03:36.470
0, 1, or 3, 0, negative 1.
Either one would be fine.

01:03:44.080 --> 01:03:47.600
We know the rank nullity theorem
tells me the null space is two

01:03:47.600 --> 01:03:51.240
dimensional, so I only need to
find two vectors.

01:03:51.240 --> 01:03:54.140
And sort of the intuitive
interpretation of this

01:03:54.140 --> 01:03:58.640
is that the dimension of the
null space

01:03:58.920 --> 01:04:02.720
is just equal to the number of
redundant columns.

01:04:02.720 --> 01:04:05.500
If d is the number of total
columns,

01:04:05.500 --> 01:04:08.960
and the rank is the number of
independent columns,

01:04:08.960 --> 01:04:11.580
the dimension of the null space
is equal to the number of

01:04:11.580 --> 01:04:13.830
unnecessary or redundant
columns.

01:04:13.980 --> 01:04:17.240
And for every one of those
unnecessary columns,

01:04:17.240 --> 01:04:19.920
there is a way to use it to make
the zero vector.

01:04:19.920 --> 01:04:22.280
Because there is a way to make
it using the other vectors you

01:04:22.280 --> 01:04:23.280
have.

01:04:24.940 --> 01:04:27.190
So the intuition,

01:04:29.040 --> 01:04:32.040
number of independent columns

01:04:33.460 --> 01:04:37.210
plus the number of dependent
columns

01:04:37.560 --> 01:04:40.580
is equal to the number of total.

01:04:40.580 --> 01:04:44.200
Now again, this is not perfect
because any column could be used

01:04:44.200 --> 01:04:48.060
in a basis, and any column could
be used to make the zero vector.

01:04:48.060 --> 01:04:51.100
So it's not like the columns are
inherently be labeled saying,

01:04:51.100 --> 01:04:52.960
this is good, this is bad.

01:04:52.960 --> 01:04:54.860
That's why this is not a precise
definition, but that's the

01:04:54.860 --> 01:04:57.860
intuition you should have.

01:04:58.060 --> 01:05:00.480
Now, these are two vectors in
the null space,

01:05:00.480 --> 01:05:02.460
and they're independent.
So we can say they're a basis

01:05:02.460 --> 01:05:04.760
for the null space.
But there are lots of other

01:05:04.760 --> 01:05:09.260
vectors in the null space, too,
like negative 1, negative 1, 1.

01:05:10.780 --> 01:05:11.840
Do you agree?

01:05:11.840 --> 01:05:14.280
The third column is just the
first plus the second.

01:05:14.280 --> 01:05:18.420
So negative 1, negative 1, 1 is
also in the null space.

01:05:18.420 --> 01:05:20.340
Do we see where that's coming
from?

01:05:20.340 --> 01:05:21.340
OK.

01:05:21.340 --> 01:05:23.420
The reason I don't need to put
that in my basis is because I

01:05:23.420 --> 01:05:26.220
can just make negative 1,
negative 1, 1 by adding the two

01:05:26.220 --> 01:05:28.280
vectors I've already collected.

01:05:28.280 --> 01:05:33.530
So that vector in the null space
has already been accounted for.

01:05:33.660 --> 01:05:34.160
OK.

01:05:34.160 --> 01:05:37.910
To connect this to previous
things we've seen,

01:05:45.040 --> 01:05:47.290
For this matrix,

01:05:47.840 --> 01:05:53.600
its column space is a blank in

01:05:53.600 --> 01:05:54.600
R2.

01:05:56.320 --> 01:06:01.840
Its row space is a blank in

01:06:01.840 --> 01:06:04.720
R...
Where does its row space live?

01:06:07.180 --> 01:06:08.680
R3, good.

01:06:09.100 --> 01:06:14.580
And its null space is a blank

01:06:14.580 --> 01:06:16.080
in R2.

01:06:17.100 --> 01:06:17.440
No,

01:06:17.440 --> 01:06:18.440
R3.

01:06:18.460 --> 01:06:22.960
What goes in each of these
blanks?

01:06:24.840 --> 01:06:28.900
Its column space is a what in
R2?

01:06:28.900 --> 01:06:32.380
English word, I'm looking for an
English word here.

01:06:32.380 --> 01:06:35.540
Column space is a what? Yeah.

01:06:35.540 --> 01:06:36.420
Not quite.

01:06:36.420 --> 01:06:39.380
You have the right idea in that
its rank is 1, so you're

01:06:39.380 --> 01:06:41.320
probably saying vector.

01:06:41.320 --> 01:06:44.000
But if I graphed its column
space,

01:06:44.000 --> 01:06:45.160
what would it look like?

01:06:45.160 --> 01:06:48.100
It's not just one vector.

01:06:48.100 --> 01:06:50.640
But what would it be?

01:06:50.640 --> 01:06:51.320
Yeah.

01:06:51.320 --> 01:06:55.820
A line of vectors, because there
are infinitely many vectors.

01:06:56.820 --> 01:06:58.640
That's what I was getting at
earlier when I said, you

01:06:58.640 --> 01:07:01.100
shouldn't think of the column
space as just being the columns

01:07:01.100 --> 01:07:03.280
that are written here.
you should think of it as all

01:07:03.280 --> 01:07:05.820
linear combinations of the
columns written here.

01:07:05.820 --> 01:07:08.260
But because the columns written
here are all scalar multiples of

01:07:08.260 --> 01:07:09.660
any one of them,

01:07:09.660 --> 01:07:10.220
really,

01:07:10.220 --> 01:07:12.680
the linear combinations of the
columns are just the scalar

01:07:12.680 --> 01:07:15.420
multiples of any one of these
three, say 1, 2,

01:07:15.420 --> 01:07:18.980
and the scalar multiples of that
one vector is a line in R2.

01:07:18.980 --> 01:07:19.400
OK,

01:07:19.400 --> 01:07:21.900
the column space of its
transpose, which is also called

01:07:21.900 --> 01:07:23.140
its row space,

01:07:23.140 --> 01:07:26.420
is a what in R3?

01:07:26.420 --> 01:07:27.920
Yeah? Yeah.

01:07:30.900 --> 01:07:32.400
Not quite.

01:07:33.020 --> 01:07:34.320
Remember, I'm asking about its
column.

01:07:34.320 --> 01:07:37.420
Sorry, I'm asking about its row
space, not the null space.

01:07:37.420 --> 01:07:38.140
The row space.

01:07:38.140 --> 01:07:39.060
Yeah, what's the row space?

01:07:39.060 --> 01:07:40.000
It would also be a line?

01:07:40.000 --> 01:07:42.250
Also a line.

01:07:43.780 --> 01:07:45.680
If the column space is a line,

01:07:45.680 --> 01:07:47.500
then the row space is also a
line,

01:07:47.500 --> 01:07:49.580
because they both have the same
dimension,

01:07:49.580 --> 01:07:52.300
which is 1 here.
Right?

01:07:52.300 --> 01:07:56.800
The column space is the line
spanned by 1, 2.

01:07:56.840 --> 01:08:00.880
The row space is the line
spanned by 1, 2, 3.

01:08:00.880 --> 01:08:04.220
It lives in a different
universe, but is also a line.

01:08:04.220 --> 01:08:06.300
Not the same line.
It's a different line, but it's

01:08:06.300 --> 01:08:07.800
a line.

01:08:08.040 --> 01:08:10.760
The answer I think you gave me
was for the null space.

01:08:10.760 --> 01:08:15.260
That is a plane in R3.

01:08:15.920 --> 01:08:16.920
OK?

01:08:17.400 --> 01:08:20.300
Now, here's a fun fact.
And this is

01:08:20.300 --> 01:08:25.060
in another one of the
supplemental videos.

01:08:25.960 --> 01:08:31.210
If this plane is the null space
of B,

01:08:35.140 --> 01:08:37.780
then this line is the row space
of B.

01:08:37.780 --> 01:08:41.360
And it turns out that the row
space and null space are

01:08:41.360 --> 01:08:43.980
perpendicular to each other.

01:08:43.980 --> 01:08:47.120
If you take any vector in the
null space,

01:08:47.120 --> 01:08:51.620
is it is perpendicular to any
vector in the row space.

01:08:52.780 --> 01:08:55.030
Why is that?

01:08:55.180 --> 01:08:58.930
Well, it's because the vectors
in the null space

01:09:00.440 --> 01:09:05.220
are made up of the v's where Bv
is equal to the 0 vector.

01:09:05.220 --> 01:09:07.400
But what is Bv doing?

01:09:07.400 --> 01:09:10.400
The way we've interpreted it is
as being linear combinations of

01:09:10.400 --> 01:09:14.680
the columns of B, but it's also
dot products with the rows of B.

01:09:14.680 --> 01:09:17.420
And what this is saying is that
any vector in the null space has

01:09:17.420 --> 01:09:19.640
to be perpendicular to every row
of B.

01:09:19.640 --> 01:09:24.480
That's what has to happen for BV
to be the zero vector.

01:09:24.480 --> 01:09:25.480
OK?

01:09:30.060 --> 01:09:35.960
V must be orthogonal to every

01:09:35.960 --> 01:09:38.210
row of B.

01:09:38.940 --> 01:09:41.160
So if it's orthogonal to every
row of B, it's orthogonal to

01:09:41.160 --> 01:09:43.900
anything you can make with the
rows of B as well.

01:09:43.900 --> 01:09:49.150
And so there's a video on this
idea

01:09:50.080 --> 01:09:53.660
about why the row space

01:09:53.660 --> 01:09:57.480
and the null space

01:09:59.860 --> 01:10:03.900
are orthogonal complements.

01:10:15.620 --> 01:10:20.100
Where we talk more about those.

01:10:20.100 --> 01:10:23.420
Do you think the column space of
B is an orthogonal complement to

01:10:23.420 --> 01:10:24.420
somebody?

01:10:27.180 --> 01:10:31.200
So far, here I've only written
out three spaces.

01:10:31.200 --> 01:10:34.200
I wrote out the column space,
the row space, the null space.

01:10:34.200 --> 01:10:36.740
Do you think there's anyone
missing?

01:10:36.740 --> 01:10:40.060
There's a fourth friend that
could enter the picture here.

01:10:40.060 --> 01:10:42.340
Column space of B and its
transpose.

01:10:42.340 --> 01:10:46.940
The null space of B, what is not
there?

01:10:46.940 --> 01:10:50.060
Yeah? The null space of B
transpose? The null space of B

01:10:50.060 --> 01:10:51.060
transpose.

01:10:59.420 --> 01:11:03.720
How do you think we fill in
these blanks?

01:11:03.720 --> 01:11:08.220
The null space of B transpose.

01:11:09.980 --> 01:11:13.260
So I'll tell you how you don't
fill in the blank.

01:11:13.260 --> 01:11:17.620
We know that the rank of B and B
transpose are the same.

01:11:17.620 --> 01:11:21.420
So you may jump to the
conclusion that the dimension of

01:11:21.420 --> 01:11:24.120
the null space of B and B
transpose are also the same.

01:11:24.120 --> 01:11:25.980
But that's not true.

01:11:25.980 --> 01:11:26.640
Instead,

01:11:26.640 --> 01:11:29.600
you have to use the rank nullity
theorem.

01:11:29.600 --> 01:11:32.800
What does the rank nullity
theorem tell us about the null

01:11:32.800 --> 01:11:35.800
space of B transpose?

01:11:38.340 --> 01:11:42.090
Well, let's write it out.

01:11:42.580 --> 01:11:48.240
It tells us the rank of B
transpose plus the dimension of

01:11:48.240 --> 01:11:52.260
the null space of B transpose

01:11:52.260 --> 01:11:55.260
is equal to what?

01:11:56.620 --> 01:11:57.620
Yeah.

01:11:57.980 --> 01:12:01.900
The number of columns that B
transpose has, which is what?

01:12:01.900 --> 01:12:02.900
Two.

01:12:03.040 --> 01:12:04.040
Exactly,

01:12:06.420 --> 01:12:08.920
which is confusing here because
if you said B is the n by d

01:12:08.920 --> 01:12:09.920
matrix,

01:12:10.020 --> 01:12:11.460
2 is n.

01:12:11.460 --> 01:12:14.080
But that's why it's about the
number of columns of the matrix

01:12:14.080 --> 01:12:17.830
who you're using the rank
nullity theorem on.

01:12:18.200 --> 01:12:23.450
But the rank of B transpose is
equal to what?

01:12:32.320 --> 01:12:33.320
1.

01:12:33.580 --> 01:12:35.940
The null space of B transpose,

01:12:35.940 --> 01:12:39.060
what is it a subspace of?

01:12:39.060 --> 01:12:43.500
We know its dimension is 1, so
it's going to be a line.

01:12:43.500 --> 01:12:48.000
But it's a line in what
universe?

01:12:48.820 --> 01:12:49.820
Remember,

01:12:52.920 --> 01:12:55.860
the null space of B transpose is
made up of vectors that I'm

01:12:55.860 --> 01:12:59.120
allowed to multiply B transpose
by.

01:12:59.120 --> 01:13:03.600
And B transpose looks like this.
1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6.

01:13:03.600 --> 01:13:05.820
So if I want to multiply it by a
vector, how many things would

01:13:05.820 --> 01:13:07.840
have to be in that vector?

01:13:07.840 --> 01:13:08.840
Two.

01:13:12.100 --> 01:13:16.580
So you can think of the row
space and the null space as

01:13:16.580 --> 01:13:18.360
existing in the same universe,

01:13:18.360 --> 01:13:22.660
and the column space and the
null space of the transpose as

01:13:22.660 --> 01:13:26.410
existing in the same universe.

01:13:26.820 --> 01:13:29.640
And while the row space and null
space are orthogonal

01:13:29.640 --> 01:13:30.640
complements,

01:13:30.960 --> 01:13:33.520
the same is also true for the
column space and the null space

01:13:33.520 --> 01:13:35.180
of the transpose.
They're also orthogonal

01:13:35.180 --> 01:13:36.180
complements.

01:13:36.840 --> 01:13:39.820
So if I were to draw out the
column space and the null space

01:13:39.820 --> 01:13:41.320
of the transpose,

01:13:41.320 --> 01:13:45.820
they would look like lines that
cross at a 90-degree angle.

01:13:50.220 --> 01:13:51.720
All right.

01:13:54.160 --> 01:13:56.960
There are some theoretical
practice activities in chapter

01:13:56.960 --> 01:13:59.220
5.4
that assess this idea.

01:13:59.220 --> 01:14:02.780
Like what is the dimension of
the null space of the transpose?

01:14:02.780 --> 01:14:07.280
And this is sometimes called the
left null space.

01:14:13.340 --> 01:14:18.590
Why is it called the left null
space?

01:14:22.320 --> 01:14:26.860
Well, it's made up of vectors
where b transpose x is equal to

01:14:26.860 --> 01:14:29.080
0.
So but is there another way you

01:14:29.080 --> 01:14:32.080
could write this expression?

01:14:33.820 --> 01:14:37.960
What would happen if I took the
transpose of this whole thing?

01:14:37.960 --> 01:14:40.960
Let's test it out.

01:14:47.400 --> 01:14:51.150
What does the left-hand side
here simplify to?

01:14:58.180 --> 01:15:00.360
Yeah? X transpose B.

01:15:00.360 --> 01:15:05.610
Good, X transpose B is equal to
the zero vector as a row vector,

01:15:05.700 --> 01:15:06.700
OK?

01:15:15.760 --> 01:15:17.940
So why is it called the left
null space?

01:15:17.940 --> 01:15:20.460
You can think of it as the null
space for vectors that you can

01:15:20.460 --> 01:15:23.720
multiply the matrix by where the
vector is on the left.

01:15:23.720 --> 01:15:25.260
But because it's on the left,

01:15:25.260 --> 01:15:29.600
for the shapes to match up, the
vector had to be rotated to be a

01:15:29.600 --> 01:15:32.180
row instead of a column.

01:15:32.180 --> 01:15:35.920
So xB is not a valid operation.

01:15:35.920 --> 01:15:37.080
But if the shapes align,

01:15:37.080 --> 01:15:39.200
x transpose B could be.

01:15:39.200 --> 01:15:43.520
But x transpose B does the same
calculation as B transpose x.

01:15:43.520 --> 01:15:45.460
The output just may be rotated

01:15:45.460 --> 01:15:48.000
as a row versus a column.

01:15:48.000 --> 01:15:51.840
So that's why things of the form
x transpose times as the matrix

01:15:51.840 --> 01:15:53.720
are often called left,

01:15:53.720 --> 01:15:56.720
like left null space.

01:15:58.680 --> 01:16:00.930
OK, let's see,

01:16:02.980 --> 01:16:05.980
one more quick activity.

01:16:10.480 --> 01:16:12.720
Find a matrix A

01:16:12.720 --> 01:16:18.400
such that, actually, before I
say that,

01:16:19.060 --> 01:16:20.680
This example B,

01:16:20.680 --> 01:16:25.580
we said that its null space is a
plane in R3.

01:16:25.580 --> 01:16:28.300
So the type of question one
could ask,

01:16:28.300 --> 01:16:32.120
and you'll see this in upcoming
homeworks and past exams,

01:16:32.120 --> 01:16:36.020
is I'm thinking of a matrix
whose null space is the plane 2x

01:16:36.020 --> 01:16:39.040
plus 5y minus 7z equals 0.

01:16:39.040 --> 01:16:41.900
And that is enough information
to know everything about the

01:16:41.900 --> 01:16:43.140
null space of the matrix,

01:16:43.140 --> 01:16:46.220
because I just gave you it in
plain form.

01:16:46.220 --> 01:16:48.440
But instead of giving you a
basis for it like this, I just

01:16:48.440 --> 01:16:50.440
gave it to you in the plane
equation.

01:16:50.440 --> 01:16:52.760
So that's the kind of thing that
may come up.

01:16:52.760 --> 01:16:55.420
Here's an equation that
describes the null space of a

01:16:55.420 --> 01:16:56.420
matrix.

01:16:56.740 --> 01:16:59.800
Now, if all you know is this,

01:16:59.800 --> 01:17:02.920
that this is a basis for its
null space,

01:17:02.920 --> 01:17:08.170
do you know the shape of the
matrix?

01:17:09.560 --> 01:17:14.810
Just with that, can you find the
shape of the matrix?

01:17:19.520 --> 01:17:20.280
Well,

01:17:20.280 --> 01:17:26.280
what do you know and what do you
not know, just from this?

01:17:28.580 --> 01:17:34.580
If you know that this is a basis
for the null space.

01:17:36.620 --> 01:17:38.660
Do you know the dimension of the
null space?

01:17:38.660 --> 01:17:40.300
Sure, that's two.

01:17:40.300 --> 01:17:43.460
Do you know the number of
columns the matrix has?

01:17:43.460 --> 01:17:45.740
Just from this.
Do you know the number of

01:17:45.740 --> 01:17:48.740
columns the matrix has?

01:17:49.040 --> 01:17:50.040
Yeah?

01:17:51.700 --> 01:17:52.700
Well,

01:17:53.640 --> 01:17:55.260
we don't yet know the rank,

01:17:55.260 --> 01:17:59.000
but we do actually know how many
columns it has.

01:17:59.000 --> 01:18:01.880
Just from this, I know whatever
matrix I'm dealing with has

01:18:01.880 --> 01:18:03.380
three columns.

01:18:03.460 --> 01:18:04.420
Why?

01:18:04.420 --> 01:18:06.940
It's because these are vectors
that I'm allowed to multiply the

01:18:06.940 --> 01:18:08.440
matrix by.

01:18:08.800 --> 01:18:11.740
These are a basis for a whole
collection of vectors that get

01:18:11.740 --> 01:18:14.480
eaten when they get multiplied
by the vector or when they get

01:18:14.480 --> 01:18:16.280
multiplied by the matrix,

01:18:16.280 --> 01:18:19.840
that means B times any one of
these equals the zero vector.

01:18:19.840 --> 01:18:22.160
But that has to mean B times any
one of these is a legal

01:18:22.160 --> 01:18:25.480
operation, so B has to have
three columns.

01:18:25.480 --> 01:18:26.840
Do we agree?

01:18:26.840 --> 01:18:27.740
So we know that.

01:18:27.740 --> 01:18:29.940
We know it's null space as
dimension 2.

01:18:29.940 --> 01:18:31.720
We know it has three columns.

01:18:31.720 --> 01:18:34.340
That means we also know the rank
of it,

01:18:34.340 --> 01:18:36.860
because we know the dimension of
its null space,

01:18:36.860 --> 01:18:38.860
and we know how many columns it
has.

01:18:38.860 --> 01:18:43.360
So what's the rank of the
matrix?

01:18:44.760 --> 01:18:47.280
Remember, rank nullity theorem
tells us rank,

01:18:47.280 --> 01:18:49.460
which we don't know yet, plus
dimension of null space, which

01:18:49.460 --> 01:18:52.380
is two, equals number of
columns, which is three.

01:18:52.380 --> 01:18:53.900
So what's its rank?

01:18:53.900 --> 01:18:54.700
One.

01:18:54.700 --> 01:18:58.260
The only thing we don't yet know
from this information alone is

01:18:58.260 --> 01:19:00.560
how many rows it has.

01:19:00.560 --> 01:19:02.820
This alone doesn't tell me how
many rows are in the matrix.

01:19:02.820 --> 01:19:03.920
There could be two,

01:19:03.920 --> 01:19:05.540
there could be 17.

01:19:05.540 --> 01:19:07.820
There needs to be at least one
because the rank of the matrix

01:19:07.820 --> 01:19:09.320
is one.

01:19:09.400 --> 01:19:12.860
If we somehow were able to
deduce that the rank was 3,

01:19:12.860 --> 01:19:14.440
it would have to have at least
three rows.

01:19:14.440 --> 01:19:17.560
Because you can't have a matrix
with two rows with a rank of 3,

01:19:17.560 --> 01:19:19.060
for example.

