It was going to be an activity
where you find a matrix where 3,

01:19:29.620 --> 01:19:32.720
1 is in the column space

01:19:32.720 --> 01:19:38.300
and 1, 3 was in its null space.

01:19:38.300 --> 01:19:39.300
OK?

01:19:39.720 --> 01:19:42.400
Notice 3, 1 and 1, 3 are not
perpendicular to each other.

01:19:42.400 --> 01:19:45.500
But nobody said they had to be.

01:19:45.500 --> 01:19:48.220
The orthogonal complement of the
null space is not the column

01:19:48.220 --> 01:19:48.960
space.

01:19:48.960 --> 01:19:51.960
It's the row space.

01:19:52.280 --> 01:19:54.920
Usually, the column space and
null space don't exist in the

01:19:54.920 --> 01:19:56.160
same universe.

01:19:56.160 --> 01:19:58.180
If you go back here,

01:19:58.180 --> 01:19:59.600
column space was in R2.

01:19:59.600 --> 01:20:01.340
Null space is in R3.

01:20:01.340 --> 01:20:03.900
But the fact that they happen to
be in the same universe here

01:20:03.900 --> 01:20:07.420
tells you what about the matrix.

01:20:07.420 --> 01:20:11.180
The column space and null space
are both in R2.

01:20:11.180 --> 01:20:16.430
It's a square 2 by 2 matrix.

